            <ArticleTitle>Tonic immobility produces hyperalgesia and antagonizes morphine analgesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>353-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hyperalgesia was demonstrated during and immediately after termination of tonic immobility in the lizard Anolis carolinensis. Additionally, tonic immobility antagonized morphine-induced analgesia. In conjunction with other research, these data suggest that the response is accompanied by a reduced availability of serotonin, possibly at postsynaptic receptors of raphe neurons.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
